  "preview": "One car's fuel economy is given as a table. The other's as an equation. Compare them — and reason about which is the more fuel-efficient car.",
  "body": {
    "goal": "Translate between two representations of a proportional relationship — a table and an equation — and use the underlying constant of proportionality to compare and predict.",
    "studentPrompt": "Two cars drive through Indiana. Their fuel economy is described in two different ways.\n\n**Car 1** — gallons used vs. miles driven:\n\n| Gallons (x) | 4 | 7 | 10 | 15 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Miles (y) | 132 | 231 | 330 | 495 |\n\n**Car 2** — described by the equation: y = 29x\n\n1. Find Car 1's miles per gallon. How do you know it's a proportional relationship?\n2. Write an equation for Car 1 in the form y = kx.\n3. Which car is more fuel-efficient? By how many miles per gallon?\n4. Each car has a 14-gallon gas tank. How much farther can the more efficient car drive on a full tank?\n5. The trip from Indianapolis to Chicago is about 185 miles. How many gallons does each car use? How much more does the inefficient car cost (gas at $3.49/gal)?",
    "image": {
      "kind": "url",
      "url": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/21811094/pexels-photo-21811094.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
      "alt": "Detailed close-up of a refueling nozzle in monochrome at a gas station, highlighting the petrol filling process.",
      "credit": "Photo by Anyana Webb on Pexels"
    },
    "launch": "Show the two representations side by side. Don't pre-explain that y = kx is the proportional form — let groups discover it from the table.",
    "materials": [
      "Vertical whiteboards",
      "Markers",
      "Calculator allowed"
    ],
    "concepts": [
      "proportional-relationships",
      "constant-of-proportionality",
      "linear-equations",
      "tables",
      "equations"
    ],
    "taskType": "performance",
    "btcFit": "Performance-style task with multiple steps. Q1-Q2 anchor the math. Q3-Q5 force comparison and real-world reasoning. The two-representation framing is the diamond — students must move between table and equation fluently.",
    "anticipatedApproaches": "- Q1: For each row, divide miles/gallons. 132/4 = 33, 231/7 = 33, 330/10 = 33, 495/15 = 33. Constant ratio confirms proportionality. Car 1 = 33 mpg.\n- Q2: y = 33x.\n- Q3: Car 1 is 33 mpg, Car 2 is 29 mpg. Car 1 is more efficient by 4 mpg.\n- Q4: Car 1 on 14 gallons: 33 × 14 = 462 miles. Car 2: 29 × 14 = 406 miles. Difference = 56 miles farther.\n- Q5: For 185 miles: Car 1 needs 185/33 ≈ 5.61 gal; Car 2 needs 185/29 ≈ 6.38 gal. Difference ≈ 0.77 gal × $3.49 ≈ $2.69 more for Car 2.",
    "commonMisconceptions": "- For Q1, computing only one row's ratio (132/4) and assuming proportionality without checking other rows.\n- Mixing up x and y: writing x = 33y instead of y = 33x.\n- For Q5, computing 185 × 33 instead of 185 ÷ 33 (multiplying when they should divide).",
    "discussionQuestions": "- How could you tell from the table that the relationship is proportional?\n- The equation y = 29x has the unit rate built right into it. Where does the 33 'live' in Car 1's representation?\n- Is 4 mpg a small difference? Plug it into Q4 and Q5. Does 4 mpg matter in real life?\n- For Q5, what if we drove 1000 miles instead? Roughly how much more would Car 2's gas cost?",
    "extensions": "- Plot both cars' fuel economy on the same coordinate plane (miles vs. gallons). What does the slope of each line represent? Why are they straight lines through the origin?\n- A third car is described in this way: 'It uses 6 gallons to drive 168 miles.' Compute its mpg. Order all three cars from most to least fuel-efficient.\n- A hybrid claims 50 mpg. Write its equation. By how much would its gas cost less than Car 1's gas for a 1,500-mile road trip?",
    "sampleSolutions": "1. Miles/gallons for each row: 33, 33, 33, 33. Constant ratio = proportional. Car 1 gets 33 mpg.\n2. y = 33x.\n3. Car 1: 33 mpg. Car 2: 29 mpg. Car 1 is more efficient by 4 mpg.\n4. Full 14-gallon tank: Car 1 → 33 × 14 = 462 miles; Car 2 → 29 × 14 = 406 miles. Difference: 56 miles farther.\n5. 185-mile trip: Car 1 → 185/33 ≈ 5.61 gal; Car 2 → 185/29 ≈ 6.38 gal. Difference: ~0.77 gal extra. Cost: 0.77 × $3.49 ≈ $2.69 more for Car 2.",
